04:32.115 --> 04:36.115
with the council and shown a kind

04:36.615 --> 04:40.286
of a rough draft, a first kind of

04:40.383 --> 04:43.432
version of that 40-year water plan.

04:43.561 --> 04:47.159
And I know they're continuing to kind of work

04:47.659 --> 04:50.748
that document out and complete

04:52.754 --> 04:56.684
it. In addition to that, we've been working, we've had

04:56.798 --> 05:00.844
meetings, gone to workshops talking

05:00.876 --> 05:05.008
about things like water conservation. And so

05:05.508 --> 05:09.291
from— we had some of our senior staff

05:09.307 --> 05:13.883
go to a meeting, a water workshop at

05:14.854 --> 05:17.442
Midway, up in Midway earlier this summer.

05:17.749 --> 05:21.455
And from that, there were a number of

05:21.877 --> 05:25.966
items that we kind of decided needed

05:26.078 --> 05:29.611
to be addressed. And one of them, and again, I think

05:30.111 --> 05:33.384
it was generally related to water conservation, but it kind of from

05:33.884 --> 05:37.527
that sprung other things we needed to do. And one of those

05:37.880 --> 05:40.176
was kind of related to this water rights code.

05:41.637 --> 05:44.511
You're familiar with John Magnus,

05:44.688 --> 05:48.124
who's kind of our legislative analyst here at the city,

05:48.397 --> 05:51.287
and he does a lot for the city. But one of the important things he

05:51.787 --> 05:54.996
does is he's actually the one who's become

05:55.061 --> 05:58.786
kind of our water rights expert. He's looking

05:58.898 --> 06:02.687
at our— he's basically keeping track

06:03.187 --> 06:06.846
of all of our water rights. You know that there's state water

06:07.346 --> 06:10.892
rights, underground water rights, and then you're familiar with our Central

06:10.941 --> 06:14.601
Utah water that we get. He's managing

06:16.253 --> 06:20.388
our water rights database, becoming familiar with both the

06:20.469 --> 06:24.039
state water rights and the Central Utah water rights

06:24.265 --> 06:27.405
and keeping track of them. So we

06:27.905 --> 06:31.293
met in the summertime and have been fleshing this out

06:31.793 --> 06:32.103
for a little while.

06:34.072 --> 06:37.582
We've had a process

06:37.711 --> 06:41.738
for accepting dedications for

06:41.819 --> 06:45.615
water, but it's kind of been You

06:46.115 --> 06:48.890
know, a lot like with a lot of the rest of the city, it needed

06:49.390 --> 06:52.246
to grow up. And, and, you know, you had, you had one person who learned

06:52.746 --> 06:55.521
how to do all of this, and, and there was never anything really,

06:56.259 --> 06:59.037
um, set in place as it should have been,

06:59.166 --> 07:02.923
like codified. And, and, uh, there weren't

07:02.939 --> 07:06.936
really solid procedures in place. And so now we're going through that process.

07:07.017 --> 07:10.960
John laid out for me some

07:11.460 --> 07:15.199
pretty solid kind of policies he wanted

07:15.699 --> 07:18.611
to operate by. And he handed me those,

07:19.431 --> 07:22.710
and the idea was that I was supposed to take those policies

07:22.967 --> 07:25.683
and make a code based on those policies.

07:26.133 --> 07:30.465
So that's what this code is before

07:30.514 --> 07:34.525
you. It's— there's 10 parts

07:35.025 --> 07:37.707
to it because we have to hit lots of different sections of the code to

07:38.207 --> 07:41.993
make it work. But for instance, we had to dive

07:42.493 --> 07:46.297
into the public utilities title

07:46.797 --> 07:50.125
of the code in Title 13. We had to hit a

07:50.417 --> 07:54.181
few places in Title 16 related to development.

07:55.610 --> 07:58.501
Well, actually, most of this is in Title 16.

08:00.124 --> 08:03.819
So we've— so there's bits

08:04.319 --> 08:08.203
and pieces of code that are going to be tweaked in existing Sections,

08:08.235 --> 08:11.817
and then we created a whole new chapter just to deal with the

08:12.269 --> 08:15.077
dedication and acceptance of water rights. So,

08:15.658 --> 08:19.170
ten parts. We're working on trying to solidify our water

08:19.528 --> 08:22.986
water rights process and to codify

08:23.034 --> 08:26.758
it. Where this code would establish

08:27.998 --> 08:31.849
a kind of a position

08:32.026 --> 08:35.878
called a water rights analyst. And John Magnus is that person

08:36.139 --> 08:37.985
who's really playing that role right now.

08:39.634 --> 08:43.155
So one of the things it does is— some of the

08:43.655 --> 08:47.710
important things that this code does is it establishes the

08:48.210 --> 08:50.298
timing of when water will be dedicated.

08:53.465 --> 08:57.230
We've had some trial and error with

08:57.730 --> 09:01.447
water rights. We've had situations where It's

09:01.639 --> 09:05.650
we'll get to the building permit stage, and we still haven't tied down water

09:06.150 --> 09:10.045
rights. And and it's it's really been a kind

09:10.061 --> 09:12.900
of a trial by fire. And so we're like we don't want to go through

09:13.400 --> 09:16.782
that experience again. So what this code would do,

09:17.360 --> 09:20.825
as as as you've looked at it, is residential water

09:21.325 --> 09:24.226
rights would be established at either through the MDA process,

09:24.900 --> 09:28.267
and if there isn't an MDA involved. then it would be established at

09:28.767 --> 09:32.626
the preliminary plat process. So there, you're— the developer would,

09:33.395 --> 09:36.151
would go through the dedication process up front.

09:36.696 --> 09:40.029
Then if they're not— if it's not residential water,

09:40.702 --> 09:44.164
then, you know, for like, uh, say we just have this Costa Vida that's

09:44.308 --> 09:47.545
just opening up, uh, if, if that were to come in

09:47.657 --> 09:51.423
after this code, they would— the code would tell us clearly that

09:51.663 --> 09:54.885
you dedicate water at the time of the building

09:54.983 --> 09:55.375
permit.

09:59.296 --> 10:01.633
And so that'd be the same for commercial,

10:02.052 --> 10:02.842
industrial,

10:06.307 --> 10:08.851
anything like institutional use.

10:09.704 --> 10:13.392
We've been meeting with Wasatch Behavioral Health. That's an example

10:13.892 --> 10:17.739
of a— and they want to establish a presence here in the community.

10:17.805 --> 10:21.365
That's an institutional use that, um, that,

10:22.023 --> 10:24.528
uh, you know, isn't commercial or, or, uh,

10:24.833 --> 10:28.558
industrial. So this code gives us kind of

10:29.058 --> 10:32.876
the timing of dedication. It is— it also— we,

10:33.165 --> 10:37.548
we even tweaked the concept plan process

10:37.741 --> 10:41.192
to— or we're proposing to tweak the concept plan process to

10:41.353 --> 10:44.869
add a requirement for a will-serve letter. It gives

10:46.667 --> 10:50.119
Public Works, the city engineer, and the water rights analyst,

10:50.184 --> 10:53.331
John Magnus, an opportunity to

10:53.831 --> 10:58.356
kind of look conceptually at a proposed development and

10:58.951 --> 11:03.302
give us an opportunity to say, yep, we will serve your development.

11:03.848 --> 11:07.300
You give us some water rights

11:07.800 --> 11:10.931
to look at and let us Give us

11:10.995 --> 11:14.190
an infrastructure plan and we'll verify that

11:14.206 --> 11:17.303
yes, there's sufficient water and infrastructure

11:17.352 --> 11:20.883
to serve your development. So that's one of the things we've been trying

11:20.916 --> 11:24.441
to do up front for a lot of just development

11:24.941 --> 11:28.080
in general is to try to get in front of the application process,

11:28.980 --> 11:32.633
have pre-application meetings. We recently

11:33.133 --> 11:36.583
updated the concept plan requirements a few months ago to

11:37.083 --> 11:40.996
require developers to give us certain information that

11:41.496 --> 11:45.442
will help us to really look at water

11:45.942 --> 11:49.308
connections, road connections. And so that's— this will-serve letter is just kind of part

11:49.808 --> 11:49.858
of that process.

11:53.508 --> 11:57.469
Um, what the Water Rights Code, it, it, uh, one of

11:57.518 --> 12:01.286
the, the main things in, in that code is it, it says—

12:01.464 --> 12:06.497
and, and this was an addit— a more recent addition from

12:06.997 --> 12:10.548
a request from John Magnus, a proposal, was that proposal to

12:10.564 --> 12:14.740
require all owners of the land and water to be applicants

12:15.240 --> 12:18.740
to an application. And that makes

12:19.240 --> 12:22.659
perfect sense to me. We don't want to have a situation

12:22.771 --> 12:26.128
where, you know, you've got an

12:26.628 --> 12:30.946
applicant and all of a sudden a water right owner gets surprised or wasn't

12:31.446 --> 12:35.547
fully aware that, you know, of all the ins and outs of a land

12:36.047 --> 12:39.459
use proposal. So we want

12:39.940 --> 12:43.595
to just get rid of any possibility of someone getting

12:43.643 --> 12:47.057
a surprise. And I've seen that before. I've seen in

12:47.557 --> 12:51.274
a planning commission meeting a property owner say, hey, I didn't know my

12:51.306 --> 12:55.634
land was proposed for a rezone. And it

12:56.134 --> 13:01.119
does happen. So So, um, so is—

13:01.232 --> 13:04.126
and I'm kind of hitting some of the high points of this code, and I'll

13:04.626 --> 13:06.907
jump into it here in a second. But, um,

13:09.575 --> 13:12.452
for a— so when a building permit comes in,

13:13.127 --> 13:16.416
the code talks about a building permit

13:16.916 --> 13:21.275
comes in, and there is— we

13:21.775 --> 13:25.683
use a software program called OpenGov And if you looked in that

13:25.795 --> 13:29.210
program, it has like a laundry list of

13:29.710 --> 13:34.062
steps that an application needs to go through. So we added a step for

13:34.272 --> 13:37.963
our water rights analyst to— and we're doing

13:38.060 --> 13:41.799
some of this now, because we have to have

13:42.299 --> 13:45.984
some process for managing water rights. But we

13:46.277 --> 13:50.343
obviously need to codify it. So we

13:50.843 --> 13:54.626
have a process for our water rights analyst Building permit

13:55.126 --> 13:59.170
comes in for a new Costa Vida. And the way this is

13:59.218 --> 14:02.847
supposed to work is that, okay, city engineer,

14:03.056 --> 14:07.070
you've got a certain number of days. You get— I

14:07.570 --> 14:11.084
can't remember. I believe it's like 3

14:11.584 --> 14:15.163
days to look at the water and determine how much

14:15.243 --> 14:21.202
water is needed for this use. And then

14:21.941 --> 14:26.986
the applicant has to come in and demonstrate that they have sufficient

14:27.067 --> 14:30.817
water to meet that calculation from the city

14:35.460 --> 14:39.182
So through the application process, if we're talking about like an MDA

14:39.682 --> 14:42.984
water right documentation, they have to submit— and we're talking about a well right— they

14:43.049 --> 14:46.632
have to submit A change application. A change application

14:46.745 --> 14:50.788
is, is an application used to change like the,

14:51.480 --> 14:54.426
the use of water or where it can be used.

14:56.550 --> 15:00.344
Um, sometimes we, we have water that, uh,

15:01.421 --> 15:04.862
maybe was— somebody's bought some water that was,

15:06.412 --> 15:10.256
you know, was pertinent to a piece of ground more

15:10.305 --> 15:12.873
on the southern end of the city. Now they want to use it more by

15:12.889 --> 15:17.229
Unity They have to go through a process to change the point of diversion,

15:17.310 --> 15:20.698
or where that water can be used. So they have to

15:20.715 --> 15:22.884
provide that type of documentation,

15:23.325 --> 15:24.842
ownership information,

15:26.955 --> 15:29.802
for the water rights analyst to review.

15:31.660 --> 15:35.321
There's a process, we spell out the process

15:35.821 --> 15:38.010
for banking water with the city.

15:40.068 --> 15:43.855
That, again, we've got our water rights analyst

15:44.355 --> 15:47.273
who's the one who manages the water,

15:47.369 --> 15:51.478
who these developers or owners will go

15:51.978 --> 15:55.297
to to work out those water right, those banking issues.

15:56.051 --> 15:59.261
We've gone through, we've spelled out

15:59.761 --> 16:03.385
the process for dedication and for keeping

16:03.497 --> 16:07.881
records of water that get banked. Banked and dedicated

16:07.929 --> 16:11.464
to the city. I'm going to dive into this in just

