32:47.806 --> 32:52.021
us requiring somebody to dedicate water rights

32:52.521 --> 32:56.541
is what's called an exaction. And so anytime you do a development exaction, you're essentially

32:57.041 --> 33:00.563
saying, because of your new development activity, we're requiring you

33:00.787 --> 33:04.729
to give us something in exchange to offset the impact

33:05.229 --> 33:08.703
of your new activity. So, you know, one type of exaction is an impact

33:09.203 --> 33:12.549
fee. Another type of exaction is you have to dedicate land for a

33:13.049 --> 33:16.298
road, things like that. So water rights are along

33:16.346 --> 33:17.661
that same type of thing.

33:18.111 --> 33:21.836
So when the new development is coming in,

33:21.964 --> 33:24.983
it is okay to exact less than the

33:25.483 --> 33:28.949
actual impact. It is not okay to exact more than the actual

33:29.449 --> 33:33.348
impact. So typically these things are a little bit conservative,

33:33.364 --> 33:37.491
and so they tend— they don't have to be like every single development application

33:38.149 --> 33:42.099
has to be exactly the right amount of water. It can be averaged out

33:42.599 --> 33:45.510
over similar types, so we don't have to say, well,

33:46.010 --> 33:50.450
every particular house is gonna use exactly 0.75 acre-feet of water.

33:50.490 --> 33:53.770
You can say, homes in general of this size with this big

33:54.270 --> 33:57.790
of a lot are gonna use this amount of water for outdoor, indoor use.

33:58.290 --> 34:00.930
And so we can do that, we can average that out. But generally speaking,

34:01.190 --> 34:03.450
you tend to be a little bit conservative with that, 'cause you don't wanna over-exact,

34:03.850 --> 34:07.970
