01:05:23.510 --> 01:05:26.781
To, um, what it was during the time we were trying to

01:05:27.281 --> 01:05:30.934
get rid of our conditional uses in the code. We brought the

01:05:31.014 --> 01:05:34.398
ag zone to you. We were cleaning up conditional uses and we

01:05:34.898 --> 01:05:39.000
were working, you know, that we've gone from conditional to special uses.

01:05:39.128 --> 01:05:42.672
And we were trying to clean up the ag zone and the

01:05:43.172 --> 01:05:46.873
special uses at the same time. What ended up happening,

01:05:47.371 --> 01:05:51.723
if you look at the ag zone currently, It just where

01:05:51.771 --> 01:05:55.205
it says public and private utilities, it's just not permitted.

01:05:55.270 --> 01:05:58.181
There, it doesn't say an S or a P.

01:05:58.746 --> 01:06:02.183
The my recollection of that that situation was

01:06:02.361 --> 01:06:05.572
it was an effort to try to stop transmission lines

01:06:05.830 --> 01:06:07.764
from going through the ag zone.

01:06:10.555 --> 01:06:13.904
But the the challenge is if if you look

01:06:14.404 --> 01:06:18.419
at the definition of utility. You, by prohibiting utilities,

01:06:18.532 --> 01:06:22.527
you pretty much prohibited everything from culinary

01:06:22.623 --> 01:06:26.080
water lines to just regular electrical lines,

01:06:26.745 --> 01:06:30.067
data transmission lines. It just,

01:06:30.148 --> 01:06:32.806
it didn't have the effect that it should have.

01:06:34.478 --> 01:06:38.058
What's more is when you look

01:06:38.558 --> 01:06:41.734
at the definition of utility in 1710 of our code,

01:06:41.815 --> 01:06:45.600
it It— the only reference it makes to,

01:06:47.260 --> 01:06:50.886
you know, power service or electrical

01:06:52.035 --> 01:06:55.935
power is it just says electrical power. It doesn't talk about—

01:06:56.178 --> 01:07:00.350
doesn't get more specific about like whether it allows for substations,

01:07:00.383 --> 01:07:02.495
transmission lines, distribution lines.

01:07:05.420 --> 01:07:08.652
So— And then if you look at like another section of the code, like you

01:07:09.152 --> 01:07:12.899
go into the business— The

01:07:13.399 --> 01:07:17.056
business office— I'm skipping or forgetting

01:07:17.088 --> 01:07:20.780
the right word here, but the collection of like

01:07:20.796 --> 01:07:24.049
business zones, like the LMD and the

01:07:24.549 --> 01:07:30.810
medical campus zone, that section of

01:07:31.310 --> 01:07:35.586
our code specifically calls out public utilities and

01:07:36.086 --> 01:07:39.330
it calls out public transmission or power transmission lines

01:07:39.830 --> 01:07:42.907
as 2 different uses. So I think there's been some intent by the city to

01:07:42.939 --> 01:07:44.438
differentiate those uses,

01:07:45.898 --> 01:07:49.063
at least at some point they have. So this code's

01:07:49.563 --> 01:07:53.362
pretty simple. It's just simple. It's simply trying— it's

01:07:53.862 --> 01:07:57.523
proposing to allow public and private utilities as

01:07:57.636 --> 01:08:01.397
a special use, and then power lines as a permitted use. And I'll

01:08:01.897 --> 01:08:05.066
explain why I did that. And And if we need to tweak that, that's fine.

01:08:05.131 --> 01:08:09.212
And I'll just tell you why. Because obviously the power transmission

01:08:09.712 --> 01:08:12.878
line is the heaviest use there.

01:08:14.540 --> 01:08:18.299
What I found when you go to Section 72, when you go to Chapter 1775,

01:08:19.510 --> 01:08:23.022
subsection 92, and you

01:08:23.522 --> 01:08:27.619
go look at the special requirements

01:08:28.119 --> 01:08:32.986
for Utilities, what you'll find is you

01:08:33.685 --> 01:08:39.020
will see requirements

01:08:39.084 --> 01:08:43.043
for general installation. You'll see requirements for substations

01:08:43.543 --> 01:08:45.101
and ground-mounted utility boxes.

01:08:47.464 --> 01:08:51.160
So there's really nothing in there about— no requirements for

01:08:51.660 --> 01:08:54.782
transmission lines except for The

01:08:54.814 --> 01:08:58.584
only thing that— there's a catch-all statement

01:08:59.084 --> 01:09:02.870
that says that any utility installation has to meet the requirements

01:09:03.370 --> 01:09:06.715
of Title 13

01:09:06.974 --> 01:09:10.465
or a specific chapter in Title 13 related to public

01:09:10.530 --> 01:09:14.536
utility installation. So that's going

01:09:15.036 --> 01:09:19.079
to be the case anyway. So what

01:09:19.579 --> 01:09:23.340
I guess I'm getting at is there really isn't anything in there Related

01:09:23.840 --> 01:09:27.507
to power transmission lines that we're trying to catch. That there's nothing

01:09:27.684 --> 01:09:33.330
in 1775 that's going to— there's

01:09:33.830 --> 01:09:37.337
no special conditions related to them. So if we— we can add it as a

01:09:37.837 --> 01:09:39.589
special use, but there's no reason to.

01:09:41.460 --> 01:09:47.339
Public and private utilities, there is a reason because ground-mounted

01:09:47.839 --> 01:09:51.367
utility boxes is probably the biggest

01:09:51.867 --> 01:09:55.298
probably the biggest example there because there's requirements related to landscaping

01:09:55.411 --> 01:10:01.368
and location and so forth. And so it makes sense to

01:10:02.997 --> 01:10:07.289
attach a special use to that use so that we can take

01:10:07.789 --> 01:10:11.695
advantage of the location and landscaping requirements and so forth. So that was kind

01:10:11.792 --> 01:10:13.008
of the thought behind it.

01:10:14.870 --> 01:10:18.538
And a little history on this, and I felt to mention this at the

01:10:19.038 --> 01:10:24.896
first, But you'll remember when we

01:10:24.976 --> 01:10:28.770
went through this process of— I spoke a lot

01:10:29.061 --> 01:10:32.785
more with the council on this, but the council had asked

01:10:33.285 --> 01:10:36.979
us to create an overlay zone. So they're like, okay, we know that transmission lines

01:10:37.011 --> 01:10:40.941
are needed. We need power to our data centers, but we want

01:10:41.441 --> 01:10:44.838
to have the ability to— we want some

01:10:44.919 --> 01:10:47.805
say in where these utility lines go,

01:10:48.356 --> 01:10:51.418
these transmission lines. And so they're like, we want an overlay zone.

01:10:53.455 --> 01:10:57.169
We found out that Spanish Fork City had thought— had

01:10:57.217 --> 01:11:00.736
similar thoughts, and it had actually adopted an overlay zone.

01:11:01.502 --> 01:11:04.774
And then they realized that they shouldn't have,

01:11:05.129 --> 01:11:08.482
or that it wasn't really doing what it was needing

01:11:08.514 --> 01:11:12.596
to do. They realized that

01:11:12.975 --> 01:11:15.095
in Chapter 15,

01:11:15.434 --> 01:11:18.444
5414 of the state code.

01:11:19.576 --> 01:11:22.943
It says that one of the first things it says is

01:11:23.041 --> 01:11:25.611
that you can't stop,

01:11:27.400 --> 01:11:32.075
you can't interfere with the utility's ability

01:11:32.253 --> 01:11:36.713
to provide cost-effective utility

01:11:37.213 --> 01:11:40.984
service. And so there's that, but it then says,

01:11:41.179 --> 01:11:44.916
okay, you can, You can place conditions on

01:11:45.333 --> 01:11:48.027
the use of utilities in your jurisdiction,

01:11:48.716 --> 01:11:52.036
but you're going to actually— you will have to pay,

01:11:53.126 --> 01:11:56.205
and it uses the term actual excess costs.

01:11:56.574 --> 01:12:01.481
So if your requirement causes the

01:12:02.171 --> 01:12:05.971
utility company to have to pay an excess cost

01:12:07.318 --> 01:12:10.008
for providing that service, city has to pay for it.

01:12:11.510 --> 01:12:14.681
So Spanish Fork's like, all right, well, we'll get out of this.

01:12:14.794 --> 01:12:18.290
And so that's— I shared that with our council, and that's

01:12:18.646 --> 01:12:21.984
kind of— they had the same reaction. They're like, well, we don't wanna

01:12:22.484 --> 01:12:26.708
