01:20:16.756 --> 01:20:20.445
the properties. So as much as we open this up to a lot more

01:20:20.979 --> 01:20:23.733
ability for people to do stuff, there's still— there's going to be so many factors

01:20:24.373 --> 01:20:26.268
based on people's lot size, they're just not going to be able to do as

01:20:26.300 --> 01:20:28.436
much because they're not going to be able to have animals,

01:20:29.656 --> 01:20:32.370
or they're just not going to have enough room to have a greenhouse.

01:20:33.365 --> 01:20:36.769
And that's just something that we can't control,

01:20:36.962 --> 01:20:40.334
but it just kind of is when you have property that's smaller. You can't

01:20:40.834 --> 01:20:45.022
always do everything that you want with it. It's not

01:20:45.038 --> 01:20:48.763
going to change anything about how animals are kept, so our animal code will still

01:20:49.263 --> 01:20:53.692
apply with how they're housed, where they're kept, the size of their living

01:20:53.790 --> 01:20:56.534
areas. All that stays the same.

01:20:57.526 --> 01:21:01.492
And then as far as processing, especially with processing

01:21:01.753 --> 01:21:05.410
animals and slaughtering of that, we just

01:21:05.910 --> 01:21:08.821
wanted to prohibit it as much as we could except for what the state already

01:21:09.321 --> 01:21:12.491
allows. So that's why that, that last line is in there.

01:21:14.150 --> 01:21:17.305
But that's about it. I don't want to go bore you with

01:21:17.805 --> 01:21:21.395
reading all of it. It's not very exhaustive, but I'm happy

01:21:21.428 --> 01:21:25.693
to answer any questions you have. Questions from the commissioner— commissioners?

01:21:26.150 --> 01:21:29.035
Okay. All right. Thank you, Steven.

